+3.  **HAVS Sorting Setup:** Configure Hardware-Assisted Visibility Sorting (HAVS) to operate in two spaces: object-space on the CPU for initial sorting and culling, and image-space on the GPU for fragment-level sorting [S1, unofficial].
+4.  **Progressive Transmission Loop:** Traverse the octree by depth ranges from front to back relative to the camera [S1, unofficial]. When the client requests a packet, the server culls faces outside the current depth range and sends the remaining faces to the client [S1, unofficial]. The depth range is incremented for future steps [S1, unofficial].
+5.  **Client-Side Rendering:** The client accumulates incoming geometry and keeps boundary triangles to maintain an interactive mode [S1, unofficial]. It refines the image using the results of previous progressive steps [S1, unofficial]. Upon completion, the client saves a copy of the image for later browsing [S1, unofficial].
 6.  **Interaction Handling:** If viewing changes (rotation, pan, zoom) occur during progressive rendering, the renderer stops and returns to interactive mode [S1, unofficial].
 7.  **Compression:** Vertex arrays are compressed using Open Source ZLIB before transmission [S1, unofficial].
